    Unified equations of state for neutron stars (Pearson+, 2018)
================================================================================
Unified equations of state for cold non-accreting neutron stars with 
Brussels-Montreal functionals. I. Role of symmetry energy
    Pearson J.M., Chamel N., Potekhin A.Y., Fantina, A.F., Ducoin C., 
    Dutta A.K., Goriely S.
================================================================================
ADC_Keywords: Models ; Pulsars
Keywords: stars: neutron - equation of state - gravitation - dense matter -
          methods: numerical

Abstract: 
    The theory of the nuclear energy-density functional is used to provide 
    a unified and thermodynamically consistent treatment of all regions of 
    neutron stars. In order to assess the impact of our incomplete knowledge 
    of the density dependence of the symmetry energy on the constitution and 
    the global structure of neutron stars, we employ four different 
    functionals. All of them were precision fitted to essentially all the 
    nuclear mass data with the Hartree-Fock-Bogoliubov method and two different 
    neutron-matter equations of state based on realistic nuclear forces. For 
    each functional, we calculate the composition, the pressure-density 
    relation, and the chemical potentials throughout the star. All our results 
    are fitted by analytic expressions. We show that uncertainties in the 
    symmetry energy can significantly affect the theoretical results for the 
    composition and observable properties of neutron stars. 
          
Description:
    Numerical results for the properties of dense matter in the inner crust 
    of cold non-accreting neutron stars using the 4th-order Extended 
    Thomas-Fermi (ETF) method with proton shell correction added perturbatively
    via the Strutinsky integral (SI). Four different functionals were used
    based on the generalized Skyrme forces BSk22, BSk24, BSk25, and BSk26. 
    The nucleon distributions were parametrized using damped Fermi profiles, 
    and the Coulomb energy was calculated within the Wigner-Seitz (WS) 
    approximation. For each mean baryon number density, the equilibrium 
    configuration was determined by minimizing the energy per nucleon with 
    respect to the numbers of neutrons and protons in the WS cell. 

    Complete results are contained in files full22.dat full24.dat full25.dat 
    and full26.dat. Equilibrium properties are summarized in files eq22.dat 
    eq24.dat eq25.dat and eq26.dat.

    
